000$. Which study provides stronger evidence against the null hypothesis?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Study B—the same difference from a larger sample is more surprising under the null hypothesis","feedback":"Correct! With $n = 2{,}000$, sample proportions vary little, so a 4-point gap is very surprising under $H_0$. With $n = 50$, such a gap could easily occur by chance."},{"correct":false,"text":"Study A—smaller samples give stronger evidence","feedback":"With only 50 observations, a 4-point difference could easily arise by random chance—it is weaker evidence."},{"correct":false,"text":"Both provide equally strong evidence, since the differences are equal","feedback":"The sample size matters a great deal: the same difference is far more surprising when it comes from a large sample."}],"multi":false,"hint":"How much do sample proportions vary from sample to sample when n is small versus large?","explanation":""},"key":"Q3OPgbBT2n"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-0e29ae0bec3a8408692689b04ac6d4d1.mjs","model":{"question":"Why can't the difference p-hat - $p_0$ serve as the test statistic by itself?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Because the same difference is more or less surprising depending on the sample size—it must be standardized","feedback":"Correct! The same raw difference means different things depending on sample-to-sample variability, so we must standardize it—accounting for the sample size—before we can judge how surprising it is."},{"correct":false,"text":"Because the difference cannot be calculated from sample data","feedback":"The difference can be computed in every study; the problem is interpreting it without a scale."},{"correct":false,"text":"Because the difference can be negative","feedback":"The sign of the difference is informative (it shows direction); the issue is that the raw size has no universal scale."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Think about what the previous question demonstrated.","explanation":""},"key":"